Layout invariants
description: the write-side checks that keep a tree well-formed, schema or no schema
- >
  The [choosing rules](::language:concretes:01-choosing) say where a value SHOULD live;
  the layout invariants assert a write actually PUT it there. They restate no policy — each
  re-asks the deriving rule and only checks that the plan obeyed the answer — and they hold
  with no schema at all, so they protect a directory from the first keystroke:
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - invariant
    - meaning
      width: 3
  - ['`nested-overlay`', 'a `.yo/` never sits inside another `.yo/`']
  - ['`reserved-overlay-name`', 'a `.yo/` holds only the body / meta / settings files, the index cache, and the fragment/thumbnail sidecars']
  - ['`escapes-root`', 'a written path stays inside the served root']
  - ['`unsafe-member-name`', 'a member name is non-empty, unpadded, not hidden, and carries no path metacharacter']
  - ['`inline-collection`', 'a child that derived to a directory encoding is written as a REAL directory, never spliced into the parent''s body']
  - ['`duplicate-member`', 'a keyed member''s key does not already name a child — the key names the node, there is no rename to fall back on']
  - ['`language-switch`', 'content spliced into a file document speaks that document''s language']
  - ['`off-scheme-name`', '(warning) a generated member carries its family''s order key']
  - ['`orphan-overlay`', 'every `.yo/` has a directory to mark and holds at least one format file']
  - ['`concrete-mismatch`', 'the concrete agrees with the shape backing it — a `dir/yamlover` owns its marker, a plain `dir` does not, a file concrete matches its extension']
- >
  Enforcement sits at the write chokepoints, before any bytes land: development and test
  builds throw, production refuses the write and logs. After the fact, the doctor endpoint
  sweeps a served tree with a **filesystem** walk — a filesystem walk because an index walk
  skips what it does not understand and so cannot see a buried overlay.
